upport-of-a-module","used_for":"Specialisation-closed support.","opened":true},{"id":"def-topological-space","used_for":"Irreducible, Noetherian, and specialisation definitions.","opened":true},{"id":"def-vanishing-ideal-affine-set","used_for":"Affine morphism and rational-map coordinate constructions.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-density-nonempty-open-affine-variety","used_for":"Dense-open equality and rational-map arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-distinguished-subset-identities","used_for":"Finite subcovers and idempotent clopens.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-maximal-ideals-are-points-over-algebraically-closed-field","used_for":"Local-ring identification at a classical point.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-minimal-prime-over-an-ideal-exists","used_for":"Declared prerequisite of the irreducible-components theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-principal-opens-form-affine-basis","used_for":"Local rings, rational-map equivalence, and indeterminacy.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-support-membership-by-annihilator","used_for":"Specialisation-closed support.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-support-of-a-cyclic-module-is-its-vanishing-set","used_for":"Nonclosed-support example.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-vanishing-set-identities","used_for":"Zariski topology and irreducible-closed-subset arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-vanishing-sets-detect-radicals","used_for":"Unique radical defining ideals.","opened":true},{"id":"prop-localisation-zero-equality-and-kernel-criteria","used_for":"Affine local-ring localization.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-affine-algebraic-sets-coordinate-duality","used_for":"Object-level affine coordinate duality.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-affine-variety-prime-coordinate-ring","used_for":"Function fields and dominant-map pullback.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-chinese-remainder-theorem-for-comaximal-ideals","used_for":"Clopen-to-idempotent construction.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-connectedness-characterisations","used_for":"Connected-spectrum criterion.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-coordinate-ring-principal-open","used_for":"Regular functions, local rings, affine opens, and rational maps.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-extension-of-scalars-is-left-adjoint-to-restriction","used_for":"Pullback-pushforward module adjunction.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-localisation-at-a-prime-is-local","used_for":"Affine local rings.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-modules-over-a-ring-form-an-abelian-category","used_for":"Stalkwise proof that sheaf categories are abelian.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-noetherian-ring-has-finitely-many-minimal-primes","used_for":"Finite irreducible components.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-noetherian-ring-ideal-characterisations","used_for":"Noetherian spectrum.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-prime-spectrum-map-by-contraction","used_for":"Contravariant spectrum functor.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-prime-spectrum-of-a-localisation-bijection","used_for":"Localization spectrum homeomorphism.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-prime-spectrum-of-a-quotient-bijection","used_for":"Quotient spectrum homeomorphism.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-proper-ideal-contained-in-maximal-ideal","used_for":"Compactness and clopen-idempotent arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-radical-as-intersection-of-primes","used_for":"Radical defining ideals.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-right-exactness-of-tensor-products","used_for":"Right exactness of module pullback.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-sheaf-morphism-isomorphism-stalkwise","used_for":"Abelian sheaves and stalkwise exactness.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-sheafification-preserves-stalks","used_for":"Stalks of inverse images, tensors, kernels, images, and cokernels.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-sheafification-universal-property","used_for":"Inverse/direct-image and pullback/pushforward adjunctions.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-support-of-arbitrary-direct-sums","used_for":"Nonclosed-support example.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"thm-global-regular-functions-affine-variety-coordinate-ring","concern":"Its sole cited input, thm-coordinate-ring-principal-open, introduces a finite-principal-cover definition of “regular on U”, whereas def-regular-function-classical-variety has already defined regularity locally on arbitrary open neighbourhoods. No bridge proves these notions agree, even for U=X; thus the cited theorem does not as written establish the stated isomorphism for this page’s existing O_X(X).","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"cex-rational-map-with-indeterminacy","concern":"The title says y/x “is undefined along x=0”, but the argument excludes extension only at (0,b) for b≠0. It neither proves nonextendability at (0,0) nor proves that the maximal domain is exactly D(x).","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-rational-maps-to-affine-variety-function-field","concern":"Step 4.1 asserts without a cited argument that a nondense image in an affine variety is annihilated by some nonzero h in k[Y]. This needs the affine closed-set/vanishing-ideal fact to justify dominance of the constructed rational map.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-birational-equivalence-function-fields","concern":"Step 1.1 applies the dominant-rational-map/function-field theorem to a birational map and its inverse without first establishing that rational maps admitting rational inverses are dominant; step 1.2 similarly uses the correspondence for the compositions without spelling out dominance.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-global-sections-left-exact","concern":"Step 1.1 says sections of the image sheaf are “exactly” the objectwise image of global sections. Objectwise images need not be sheaves. The proof should instead use that the initial map is monic and identifies F' with the kernel sheaf, then take sections.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-clopen-subset-gives-idempotent-decomposition","concern":"In step 4.1, from e∈p and e(1-e)=0 it says primality implies 1-e∉p. The conclusion is true because p is proper and otherwise 1=e+(1-e) lies in p, but it does not follow from primality as stated.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"}],"alerts":[{"item":"thm-coordinate-ring-principal-open","finding":"Opened as a direct dependency of multiple owned affine-variety items. It says, “A function φ:U→k is called regular on U if there exist finitely many pairs ... U=⋃D_X(h_i) ...”, although def-regular-function-classical-variety already defines regularity locally on arbitrary open neighbourhoods. Its proof proves the localization statement only for this newly finite-cover notion and supplies no equivalence with the pre-existing definition, so downstream citations to it as a statement about the established O_X(U) are unsupported.","severity":"would-be-fatal"}],"seams_checked":[]}